1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,400
[Night. Lamplight. A hand in a brown wool cuff taps a Morse key: one… two-two… three-three-three.]

2
00:00:02,550 --> 00:00:05,435
[A sunlit kitchen. Kai yawns over a mug. Mira draws at the table. Biscuit sits very politely beside the toast.]

3
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,315
NARRATOR: I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

4
00:00:05,585 --> 00:00:08,735
[Close on Mira. She puts her crayon down, picks up her drawing of the Sun and holds it up, proud.]

5
00:00:06,535 --> 00:00:09,730
NARRATOR: I was drawing the Sun. Of course I was.

6
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:13,130
[Kai, sleepy, squints at her over his mug.]

7
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,010
NARRATOR: Who draws at six in the morning?

8
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:15,130
[Mira, admiring her own work.]

9
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:14,540
MIRA: Artists.

10
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:18,075
[Close on Biscuit. His eyes slide slowly toward the toast on the edge of the table.]

11
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:17,955
NARRATOR: And Biscuit was planning a crime.

12
00:00:18,225 --> 00:00:21,375
[Sydney, mid-afternoon. A surfer stands up on the wave of her life.]

13
00:00:18,325 --> 00:00:21,660
NARRATOR: At that moment, half the world was in daylight.

14
00:00:21,930 --> 00:00:23,780
[A paddy field before noon. A farmer straightens up and squints at the sky.]

15
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:26,780
[A boy sprints for his school bus. The doors close on his nose and it pulls away without him.]

16
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:28,780
[Back in the kitchen, Biscuit rises on his back legs and snatches the toast. Kai points, outraged.]

17
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:28,340
NARRATOR: Biscuit, no!

18
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:32,080
[Mid-snatch, the sunlight drains out of the room from the window. Everyone freezes in the blue dark. Biscuit still has the toast.]

19
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:31,495
NARRATOR: And then the Sun went out.

20
00:00:32,365 --> 00:00:33,315
[Sydney: the same wave, the same surfer, under sudden stars.]

21
00:00:33,465 --> 00:00:34,415
[Chennai: the paddy goes black before noon.]

22
00:00:34,565 --> 00:00:35,515
[Manila: the boy at the empty bus stop, in the dark.]

23
00:00:35,665 --> 00:00:38,715
[At the window: stars over the city at breakfast time. Mira folds her drawing into her pocket.]

24
00:00:35,765 --> 00:00:37,445
MIRA: Is it night again?

25
00:00:38,865 --> 00:00:42,015
[Kai turns from the window and puts his mug down on the table, without looking at it.]

26
00:00:38,965 --> 00:00:41,530
NARRATOR: It can't be night. It's breakfast time.

27
00:00:42,265 --> 00:00:45,415
[Kai flicks the light switch. The kitchen lamp comes on, warm and ordinary. The window stays full of stars.]

28
00:00:43,915 --> 00:00:46,705
NARRATOR: The lights still worked. The Sun didn't.

29
00:00:46,975 --> 00:00:50,125
[Three hard knocks rattle the front door. Kai goes to the door and opens it.]

30
00:00:47,075 --> 00:00:49,795
NARRATOR: Then someone knocked. Three times.

31
00:00:50,575 --> 00:00:53,725
[Kai holds the door open on Mr. Ito from upstairs: fully dressed, shoes on, a torch in one hand and a packed canvas bag in the other.]

32
00:00:50,675 --> 00:00:55,065
MR. ITO: Good morning, you two. Your mum just phoned from the hospital.

33
00:00:55,335 --> 00:00:58,485
[Reverse: ito answers.]

34
00:00:55,435 --> 00:00:58,405
MR. ITO: She's all right. You're with me today.

35
00:00:58,675 --> 00:01:01,825
[Ito, calm, nods at their bare feet.]

36
00:00:58,775 --> 00:01:02,320
MR. ITO: Put your shoes on, please. Both of you.

37
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:05,170
[Close on Ito, calm in the torchlight.]

38
00:01:02,690 --> 00:01:05,050
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito, from upstairs.

39
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:08,470
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

40
00:01:05,420 --> 00:01:08,880
NARRATOR: He was seventy-three, and he was never in a hurry.

41
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:12,300
[Close on the canvas bag in his hand: worn straps, a luggage tag, packed tight.]

42
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:13,340
NARRATOR: His bag was already packed. It had been packed for years.

43
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:16,760
[Ito nods past them at Biscuit, who still has the toast in his mouth.]

44
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:16,955
MR. ITO: And bring the dog. He has your toast.

45
00:01:17,225 --> 00:01:20,375
[Title: THE SUN WENT OUT.]

46
00:01:17,325 --> 00:01:19,890
NARRATOR: It took me a year to understand why.

47
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:24,070
[Outside, the street is full. Pyjamas and work clothes, every phone held up to a starry sky. Kai reads a message on his phone.]

48
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:23,950
NARRATOR: Outside, the whole street was looking up.

49
00:01:24,220 --> 00:01:27,370
[Close on Kai’s phone: a text from Mum.]

50
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:27,290
NARRATOR: Mum was a nurse. She couldn't come home.

51
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:29,670
[A man in a dressing gown shouts at the sky.]

52
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:29,430
NARRATOR: It's just an eclipse!

53
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:32,970
[Ito, unimpressed, to Mira.]

54
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:34,435
MR. ITO: An eclipse never lasts more than seven and a half minutes.

55
00:01:34,705 --> 00:01:37,855
[On every phone in the street: a news channel, four live feeds. Tokyo crowds in the dark at 2:52 PM. Cairo. A grounded airport. New York, asleep.]

56
00:01:34,805 --> 00:01:38,305
NARRATOR: It was happening everywhere, at the same moment.

57
00:01:38,875 --> 00:01:42,025
[The anchor, and beside her a feed of New York at night: dark windows, a city asleep.]

58
00:01:38,975 --> 00:01:41,685
NARRATOR: In New York, it was two in the morning.

59
00:01:42,255 --> 00:01:45,405
[The feeds again, hours later: alarm clocks ringing in black bedrooms, crowds in the streets.]

60
00:01:42,355 --> 00:01:46,210
NARRATOR: Most of them woke up to a morning that never came.

61
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:50,030
[Ito turns to Kai and nods up at the roof of their building.]

62
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:50,700
MR. ITO: Up to the roof. Kai, bring my telescope.

63
00:01:50,970 --> 00:01:54,120
[The stairwell, sixth floor. Ito climbs ahead with the torch. Kai hauls the heavy telescope case, puffing. Mira skips behind.]

64
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:55,280
NARRATOR: The lift was full of neighbours, so Kai carried it up six floors.

65
00:01:55,550 --> 00:01:58,130
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

66
00:01:55,650 --> 00:01:58,010
NARRATOR: He complained about every one.

67
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:01,430
[The roof. Ito sets his bag down; Kai drops the case with a groan. Ito looks east, where the Sun rose an hour ago.]

68
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:02,815
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito came up here every morning to watch the sunrise.

69
00:02:03,085 --> 00:02:06,235
[He sets the torch on the ground and builds the brass telescope with quick, sure hands.]

70
00:02:03,185 --> 00:02:06,040
NARRATOR: That morning, he'd seen the last one.

71
00:02:06,610 --> 00:02:09,460
[Reverse: ito answers.]

72
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:09,340
MR. ITO: First, let's find Venus.

73
00:02:09,610 --> 00:02:11,965
[Low in the east, one bright point still shining: Venus.]

74
00:02:09,710 --> 00:02:11,845
MIRA: Why is that star still shining?

75
00:02:12,115 --> 00:02:14,075
[Diagram: the Sun as a dark question mark, Venus catching its light and passing it on to Earth.]

76
00:02:12,215 --> 00:02:13,955
MR. ITO: That's Venus.

77
00:02:14,225 --> 00:02:17,375
[Continues.]

78
00:02:14,325 --> 00:02:21,040
MR. ITO: It shines with sunlight bouncing off it, and that light takes the long way round to reach us.

79
00:02:21,310 --> 00:02:24,460
[Ito, eyes on his watch.]

80
00:02:21,410 --> 00:02:26,815
MR. ITO: If something's happened to the Sun itself, Venus will go dark too.

81
00:02:27,085 --> 00:02:29,820
[Reverse: ito answers.]

82
00:02:27,185 --> 00:02:29,700
MR. ITO: At four minutes past seven.

83
00:02:29,970 --> 00:02:32,630
[They sit on the roof and wait. Kai checks his watch: 7:03.]

84
00:02:30,070 --> 00:02:31,510
NARRATOR: So we waited.

85
00:02:32,780 --> 00:02:35,030
[At four minutes past seven, Venus flickers and goes out.]

86
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:38,330
[Ito, very quietly.]

87
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:39,170
MR. ITO: There it goes. So it's the Sun itself.

88
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,990
[Diagram: Jupiter, far out. A long light path. A clock.]

89
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:43,005
MR. ITO: Jupiter's much further away.

90
00:02:43,275 --> 00:02:46,425
[Continues.]

91
00:02:43,375 --> 00:02:46,915
MR. ITO: Its light should go out at four minutes past eight.

92
00:02:47,185 --> 00:02:50,335
[Jupiter, centred in the telescope crosshairs, winks out.]

93
00:02:47,285 --> 00:02:50,065
NARRATOR: At four minutes past eight, it did.

94
00:02:50,735 --> 00:02:53,495
[Ito taps the telescope three times, the way he always does.]

95
00:02:50,835 --> 00:02:53,375
NARRATOR: He tapped the telescope three times.

96
00:02:53,645 --> 00:02:56,795
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

97
00:02:53,745 --> 00:02:56,865
NARRATOR: He always did that. I never asked why.

98
00:02:57,135 --> 00:02:59,985
[A small dome on a dark mountain. Beside it a radio dish turns toward one bright star.]

99
00:03:00,135 --> 00:03:02,785
[A small observatory at night. A young man with round glasses bends over a light table.]

100
00:03:02,935 --> 00:03:05,685
[An old eclipse plate under a magnifier. One faint line through the corona, far too straight. A red pen circles it.]

101
00:03:05,835 --> 00:03:08,395
[Close on Kai’s phone: a government EMERGENCY ALERT.]

102
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:08,275
(RADIO): Stay indoors and keep warm.

103
00:03:08,545 --> 00:03:11,600
[Continues.]

104
00:03:08,645 --> 00:03:11,480
(RADIO): Power and water are working normally.

105
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:14,130
[Kai, already pulling on his shoes.]

106
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:14,010
NARRATOR: Everyone's going to the shops.

107
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:16,130
[Reverse: kai answers.]

108
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:15,845
NARRATOR: We should go too.

109
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:19,430
[Ito takes a folded list out of his bag and hands it to Kai.]

110
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:19,975
MR. ITO: I'm ahead of you. Here's the list.

111
00:03:20,245 --> 00:03:23,395
[The whole street runs the same way, in summer clothes under streetlights: toward the supermarket.]

112
00:03:20,345 --> 00:03:23,890
NARRATOR: Kai was right. The whole city had the same idea.

113
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:26,870
[Half-empty shelves. Kai steers a trolley, reading Ito’s list.]

114
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:26,750
NARRATOR: Rice. Tins. Warm socks. Seeds?

115
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:30,085
[Mira marches back with an armful of dog treats. Biscuit loses his mind.]

116
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:29,965
MIRA: And dog treats. They're on Biscuit's list.

117
00:03:30,235 --> 00:03:33,385
[Two grown men in a tug-of-war over the last electric heater.]

118
00:03:30,335 --> 00:03:33,845
NARRATOR: Two grown men fought over the last electric heater.

119
00:03:34,115 --> 00:03:37,265
[Ito watches the fight, deadpan, and turns back to Kai’s trolley of rice, tins and seed packets.]

120
00:03:34,215 --> 00:03:37,585
MR. ITO: Heat won't be the hard part. Food will.

121
00:03:37,855 --> 00:03:41,005
[Reverse: ito answers.]

122
00:03:37,955 --> 00:03:40,920
MR. ITO: Nothing grows without the Sun.

123
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:44,265
[Day 0, evening. A hospital corridor turned shelter. At the nurses’ station, a nurse in scrubs holds up her phone for a video call.]

124
00:03:41,290 --> 00:03:44,145
NARRATOR: That evening, at seven, Mum called.

125
00:03:44,415 --> 00:03:47,160
[Reverse: mum answers.]

126
00:03:44,515 --> 00:03:47,040
NARRATOR: Kai! Mira! Oh, there you are.

127
00:03:47,310 --> 00:03:49,675
[Their flat, that evening. Kai holds his phone up for the video call; Mira squeezes in beside him.]

128
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,555
NARRATOR: Mum! Are you okay?

129
00:03:49,825 --> 00:03:52,815
[Mum, tired, smiling at the sight of them on her screen.]

130
00:03:49,925 --> 00:03:52,695
NARRATOR: I'm fine, love. I'm so sorry.

131
00:03:52,965 --> 00:03:56,115
[Reverse: mum answers.]

132
00:03:53,065 --> 00:03:56,355
NARRATOR: I have to stay here. They need every nurse.

133
00:03:56,625 --> 00:03:59,775
[Wide: every bed full, cots lining the corridor; an ambulance’s blue light sweeps through the doors.]

134
00:03:56,725 --> 00:04:00,390
NARRATOR: The ambulances haven't stopped since it went dark.

135
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:02,545
[Mira leans in to the phone in Kai’s hand.]

136
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:02,425
MIRA: When are you coming home?

137
00:04:02,695 --> 00:04:05,845
[Mum closes her eyes for a second, then makes her voice bright.]

138
00:04:02,795 --> 00:04:07,465
NARRATOR: As soon as I can, sweetheart. I'll call every night at seven.

139
00:04:07,735 --> 00:04:10,885
[Reverse: mum answers.]

140
00:04:07,835 --> 00:04:10,900
NARRATOR: Be good for Mr. Ito. I love you.

141
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:14,320
[Kai ends the call and pockets his phone. Ito, by the window, watching them.]

142
00:04:11,270 --> 00:04:14,310
NARRATOR: And she did call. Every single night.

143
00:04:14,580 --> 00:04:17,515
[Ito sets his packed bag down by the sofa: he is staying.]

144
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:17,395
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito slept on our sofa.

145
00:04:17,665 --> 00:04:20,110
[Later. Kai and Mira on the rug. Kai finally asks it.]

146
00:04:17,765 --> 00:04:19,690
NARRATOR: Is the Sun dead?

147
00:04:20,260 --> 00:04:22,650
[Diagram: the Moon tugs a bulge of ocean toward it. The hidden Sun tugs too.]

148
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:22,530
MR. ITO: No. I don't think so.

149
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:25,950
[Continues.]

150
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:27,190
MR. ITO: If it had vanished, its pull on the sea would vanish too.

151
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:30,610
[The Sun’s bulge is removed: the tide shrinks by a third.]

152
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:31,550
MR. ITO: And tomorrow's high tide would be a third lower.

153
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:34,970
[Ito takes an old tide table out of his bag and hands it to Kai.]

154
00:04:32,670 --> 00:04:39,165
MR. ITO: Here's a tide table. Go and see for yourselves. High tide is at twenty to eleven.

155
00:04:39,435 --> 00:04:42,585
[Day 1. The harbour. Torchlight on boats and black water.]

156
00:04:39,535 --> 00:04:43,325
NARRATOR: So the next morning, Kai and I walked down to the harbour.

157
00:04:43,595 --> 00:04:45,450
[The torch finds the painted tide gauge. The water sits exactly on Ito’s pencilled mark.]

158
00:04:43,695 --> 00:04:45,330
MIRA: It's right on the line!

159
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:47,785
[Kai stares at the tide table, then up.]

160
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:47,665
NARRATOR: So the Sun's still there.

161
00:04:47,935 --> 00:04:49,915
[Reverse: kai answers.]

162
00:04:48,035 --> 00:04:49,795
NARRATOR: We just can't see it.

163
00:04:50,065 --> 00:04:52,120
[Mira looks up at the black sky.]

164
00:04:50,165 --> 00:04:51,700
MIRA: Then why is it dark?

165
00:04:52,270 --> 00:04:55,420
[The two of them, tiny at the edge of the freezing harbour, staring at where the Sun should be.]

166
00:04:52,370 --> 00:04:56,490
NARRATOR: The Sun hadn't gone anywhere. Something was in front of it.

167
00:04:57,260 --> 00:04:59,520
[Mira’s mitten points down at the dark water: a fish glides past in the torch beam, unbothered.]

168
00:04:57,360 --> 00:04:59,400
MIRA: The fish haven't even noticed!

169
00:04:59,670 --> 00:05:02,820
[Day 2. A press conference. Behind the scientist, an infrared image: a warm ring where the Sun should be.]

170
00:04:59,770 --> 00:05:03,360
NEWS ANCHOR: Infrared telescopes have found something around the Sun.

171
00:05:03,630 --> 00:05:05,890
[The scientist at the podium, the warm ring behind her.]

172
00:05:03,730 --> 00:05:05,770
NARRATOR: It's warm. It's round.

173
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:09,190
[Continues.]

174
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:08,905
NARRATOR: And it's twice as wide as the Sun.

175
00:05:09,475 --> 00:05:12,140
[Pull back from where the Sun should be: a colossal sphere of dark slats wrapped around the star, faintly glowing at the seams.]

176
00:05:09,575 --> 00:05:12,020
NARRATOR: Not a cloud. Not a planet.

177
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:15,440
[The camera glides around the shell. Each of its slats is wider than Earth.]

178
00:05:12,390 --> 00:05:14,910
NARRATOR: A shell. Wrapped around a star.

179
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:19,130
[The feeds: candlelit prayer in Rome, protests in London, radar rooms, a stampede for rice in Mumbai.]

180
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:19,220
NEWS ANCHOR: Governments are asking everyone to stay calm.

181
00:05:19,490 --> 00:05:21,750
[Continues.]

182
00:05:19,590 --> 00:05:21,230
NARRATOR: Nobody was calm.

183
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:24,360
[Their flat. The news on the little TV. Mira turns from it to Mr. Ito.]

184
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,240
MIRA: Mr. Ito? Who built it?

185
00:05:24,510 --> 00:05:27,660
[Ito crosses to the TV and switches it off. He does not look at her.]

186
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:28,300
MR. ITO: It's bedtime, Mira.

187
00:05:28,570 --> 00:05:31,205
[Mira, in the dark. The clock says four in the afternoon.]

188
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:30,785
NARRATOR: It was four in the afternoon.

189
00:05:31,355 --> 00:05:34,505
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

190
00:05:31,455 --> 00:05:34,070
NARRATOR: He'd never sent me to bed before.

191
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:37,890
[The whole sky ignites: green and violet curtains over the frozen city.]

192
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:39,530
NEWS ANCHOR: A solar storm that left the Sun before it went dark is reaching Earth tonight.

193
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:42,575
[Mira, face lit green, watching.]

194
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:41,855
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

195
00:05:42,725 --> 00:05:45,575
[The young astronomer studies three plates pinned to the wall beside a star chart.]

196
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:48,775
[Red lines, extended from each plate, meet at one faint star on the chart. He writes 21 beside it.]

197
00:05:48,925 --> 00:05:52,075
[Day 7. The city from above, every window still lit, frost rolling over the rooftops.]

198
00:05:49,025 --> 00:05:51,965
NARRATOR: By Day 7, it was twenty below.

199
00:05:52,235 --> 00:05:55,385
[Close on a frozen pipe in their building: split open, an icicle hanging from the crack.]

200
00:05:52,335 --> 00:05:57,665
NARRATOR: The pipes in our building froze and burst, and the radiators went cold.

201
00:05:57,935 --> 00:06:01,085
[Earth from orbit: ice spreading toward the equator, the cities’ lights still on.]

202
00:05:58,035 --> 00:06:02,370
NARRATOR: All over the world, heating was never built for cold like this.

203
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:05,790
[Blankets taped over the windows of one room. Mira holds the corner while Kai tapes.]

204
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:06,225
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito showed us how to keep one room warm.

205
00:06:06,495 --> 00:06:09,645
[The warm room, lit by the lamp and the small electric heater. Kai sits up straight, trying to look calm.]

206
00:06:06,595 --> 00:06:09,735
NARRATOR: That night, Kai pretended he wasn't scared.

207
00:06:10,005 --> 00:06:13,155
[Close, in the heater’s glow: Kai’s hand clamped round Mira’s mitten, knuckles pale.]

208
00:06:10,105 --> 00:06:12,810
NARRATOR: He was holding my hand too hard.

209
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,730
[Torch beam in the stairwell. Grey smoke curls out from under a neighbour’s door.]

210
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:16,805
NARRATOR: Then, out on the stairs, we smelled smoke.

211
00:06:17,075 --> 00:06:20,225
[The torch finds door 3B: firelight flickering underneath, smoke pouring out.]

212
00:06:17,175 --> 00:06:20,165
MR. ITO: Next door's heating is off too.

213
00:06:20,435 --> 00:06:23,585
[Reverse: ito answers.]

214
00:06:20,535 --> 00:06:23,700
MR. ITO: They're burning furniture. Indoors!

215
00:06:23,970 --> 00:06:25,820
[Ito, in the smoke, suddenly very loud.]

216
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:25,635
MR. ITO: Everyone out!

217
00:06:25,970 --> 00:06:29,120
[Closer on Ito, urgent, explaining as he herds them toward the stairs.]

218
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:29,510
MR. ITO: A fire indoors makes carbon monoxide.

219
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:32,930
[Reverse: ito answers.]

220
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:33,685
MR. ITO: You can't see it, you can't smell it, and it kills.

221
00:06:33,955 --> 00:06:36,760
[Down the stairs: neighbours coughing into their sleeves, Kai with the torch, Mira at his heels, Ito banging on doors, Biscuit ahead.]

222
00:06:34,055 --> 00:06:36,640
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

223
00:06:36,910 --> 00:06:39,880
[The street door bangs open into the week’s big snowstorm. Biscuit bolts into the dark. Kai lunges, torch swinging.]

224
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:39,760
NARRATOR: At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

225
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:42,335
[Mira screams his name into the storm. Nothing comes back.]

226
00:06:40,130 --> 00:06:41,215
MIRA: BISCUIT!

227
00:06:42,485 --> 00:06:45,075
[That night. Kai walks the snowy street alone with a torch, streetlights glowing through the snow.]

228
00:06:42,585 --> 00:06:44,955
NARRATOR: Biscuit! Come on, boy! Please!

229
00:06:45,225 --> 00:06:48,160
[Kai turns on the spot, sweeping the torch. Nothing.]

230
00:06:45,325 --> 00:06:48,040
NARRATOR: The fire brigade put the fire out.

231
00:06:48,310 --> 00:06:51,460
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

232
00:06:48,410 --> 00:06:52,035
NARRATOR: Kai searched all night, and came back without him.

233
00:06:52,305 --> 00:06:55,405
[Day 8, seven in the evening. Mum on the nightly video call, in the same scrubs, more tired.]

234
00:06:52,405 --> 00:06:55,285
NARRATOR: Mira, sweetheart, listen to me.

235
00:06:55,555 --> 00:06:58,290
[Reverse: mum answers.]

236
00:06:55,655 --> 00:06:58,170
NARRATOR: Biscuit is a clever dog.

237
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:01,590
[In the warm room, Mira holds Kai’s phone in both hands, listening to Mum.]

238
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:01,545
NARRATOR: Clever dogs find warm places.

239
00:07:02,215 --> 00:07:04,775
[Mira turns to Kai.]

240
00:07:02,315 --> 00:07:04,655
MIRA: Kai, where would it still be warm?

241
00:07:04,925 --> 00:07:07,885
[Reverse: kai answers.]

242
00:07:05,025 --> 00:07:07,465
NARRATOR: I don't know. But I'm going to find him.

243
00:07:08,035 --> 00:07:10,485
[Kai, back out on the frosty street. Somewhere near the metro, a single bark (a little "WOOF!" pops out of the dark). His head snaps round.]

244
00:07:08,135 --> 00:07:10,095
NARRATOR: Kai went straight back out.

245
00:07:10,635 --> 00:07:13,095
[The torch beam finds a line of fresh paw prints in the snow, toes pointing toward the metro.]

246
00:07:10,735 --> 00:07:12,975
NARRATOR: Paw prints. Fresh ones.

247
00:07:13,245 --> 00:07:16,395
[The prints lead across the street to a cloud of steam rising from a metro entrance.]

248
00:07:13,345 --> 00:07:18,700
NARRATOR: They led to the only warm thing on the street: air rising from the metro.

249
00:07:18,970 --> 00:07:22,120
[Biscuit is curled against the warm grate, frost on his whiskers. He sees Kai. His tail explodes. Kai drops the torch in the snow and grabs him.]

250
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:23,685
NARRATOR: You clever, clever dog. You scared me.

251
00:07:23,955 --> 00:07:27,105
[Cut-away of the ground: frozen surface, then the metro tunnels, then warm rock below.]

252
00:07:24,055 --> 00:07:29,805
NARRATOR: A few metres down, the ground stays at about ten degrees, Sun or no Sun.

253
00:07:30,075 --> 00:07:33,225
[Kai picks up his torch. Biscuit trots toward the metro stairs and looks back. Come on.]

254
00:07:30,175 --> 00:07:33,790
NARRATOR: Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd found the way down.

255
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:37,210
[Down the metro stairs: Kai carrying Mira on his back, Ito with his bag, and behind them a line of torches.]

256
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:37,370
NARRATOR: By morning, half the street had followed him down.

257
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:40,790
[Feeds from around the world: tents in metro halls, families on escalators, shelters cut into rock.]

258
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:41,420
NARRATOR: All over the world, cities moved underground.

259
00:07:41,690 --> 00:07:44,840
[Day 30. The metro is a town: tents on the platforms, string lights, laundry lines.]

260
00:07:41,790 --> 00:07:45,575
NARRATOR: By Day 30, Harbour Street station was a town.

261
00:07:45,845 --> 00:07:48,995
[The station’s big screen: a power station steaming, an electric freight train, someone working from home, an underground farm.]

262
00:07:45,945 --> 00:07:50,655
NARRATOR: The power stations kept running, on coal, gas and nuclear.

263
00:07:50,925 --> 00:07:54,075
[Electric freight trains with containers of FOOD and FUEL, and power stations steaming.]

264
00:07:51,025 --> 00:07:53,960
NARRATOR: Electric trains carried food and fuel.

265
00:07:54,230 --> 00:07:57,380
[More feeds: home offices, a school in a shelter, farms under lamps, freight trains.]

266
00:07:54,330 --> 00:07:59,270
NARRATOR: Anyone who could work from home, did. Everyone else worked underground.

267
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:02,690
[A disused tunnel turned farm: racks of lettuce and potatoes under pink lamps, and crates of mushrooms on an unlit shelf. Labels on every rack.]

268
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:03,605
NARRATOR: We grew lettuce, because it's ready in a month. Potatoes.

269
00:08:03,955 --> 00:08:07,065
NARRATOR: And mushrooms, which don't need any light at all.

270
00:08:07,335 --> 00:08:10,140
[A small kid glares at the chalk menu: lettuce, potatoes, lettuce, mushrooms, …lettuce.]

271
00:08:07,435 --> 00:08:10,020
NARRATOR: Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

272
00:08:10,290 --> 00:08:13,440
[Seven a.m. The big lamps in the station roof warm up from orange to gold.]

273
00:08:10,390 --> 00:08:14,775
NARRATOR: At seven every morning, the engineers turned on the big lamps.

274
00:08:15,045 --> 00:08:17,645
[Faces turned up into the gold light. Mira jumps. Ito smiles.]

275
00:08:15,145 --> 00:08:17,525
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

276
00:08:17,795 --> 00:08:20,945
[In a parked carriage turned classroom, Mira (the tallest child in the room) shows four much smaller kids her drawing from the last morning.]

277
00:08:18,645 --> 00:08:22,385
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And it's warm on your face.

278
00:08:22,655 --> 00:08:25,805
[A small kid holds up her own drawing: a grey sun.]

279
00:08:22,755 --> 00:08:26,020
NARRATOR: The littlest ones had started drawing it grey.

280
00:08:26,890 --> 00:08:30,040
[Mira looks at the grey sun, then folds her own drawing carefully back into her pocket.]

281
00:08:26,990 --> 00:08:30,410
NARRATOR: I kept my drawing in my pocket. All year.

282
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:34,230
[Biscuit, noticeably rounder, receives snacks from a ring of adoring kids.]

283
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:34,425
NARRATOR: Biscuit got a fan club. And a bigger belly.

284
00:08:34,695 --> 00:08:37,845
[A new sign goes up on the platform wall: CLINIC.]

285
00:08:34,795 --> 00:08:38,630
NARRATOR: That month, the hospital opened a clinic in our station.

286
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:40,960
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

287
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,840
NARRATOR: Mum asked to work there.

288
00:08:41,110 --> 00:08:44,260
[A woman in a navy coat over nurse’s scrubs walks along the platform with her bag, past the new CLINIC sign. She sets the bag down. Mira and Kai run.]

289
00:08:41,210 --> 00:08:42,100
MIRA: Mum!

290
00:08:44,510 --> 00:08:47,455
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

291
00:08:44,610 --> 00:08:46,535
NARRATOR: And then she was home.

292
00:08:47,605 --> 00:08:50,570
[Mum turns to Ito, who has kept the children safe for a month.]

293
00:08:47,705 --> 00:08:50,450
NARRATOR: Thank you. For keeping them alive.

294
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:53,870
[Reverse: mum answers.]

295
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:54,025
NARRATOR: For looking after them, all this time.

296
00:08:54,295 --> 00:08:57,445
[Ito, quietly.]

297
00:08:54,395 --> 00:08:57,410
MR. ITO: No. They kept me alive.

298
00:08:58,080 --> 00:09:00,930
[A typed letter under the lamp: the Council cannot support further observations, nor any transmission. His thumb covers the name.]

299
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:04,130
[The receiver at his ear. Down the line, a colleague laughs. He says nothing.]

300
00:09:04,280 --> 00:09:07,265
[Day 40. Ito’s workbench in the station, his radio and his bag on it. Mira asks the question he would not answer.]

301
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:07,145
MIRA: Mr. Ito, you never answered me.

302
00:09:07,415 --> 00:09:09,265
[Reverse: mira answers.]

303
00:09:07,515 --> 00:09:08,800
MIRA: Who built it?

304
00:09:09,415 --> 00:09:12,565
[Kai, backing her up.]

305
00:09:09,515 --> 00:09:12,540
NARRATOR: Whoever it was, it must have taken them ages.

306
00:09:12,810 --> 00:09:15,960
[Ito turns to the bag on his bench and takes out an old photograph.]

307
00:09:12,910 --> 00:09:16,885
MR. ITO: Much longer than that. It's very old. Here.

308
00:09:17,455 --> 00:09:20,605
[An old eclipse photograph in Ito’s hand: three faint, perfectly straight lines crossing the corona.]

309
00:09:17,555 --> 00:09:22,275
MR. ITO: Nineteen-nineteen. A total eclipse. Look at the lines.

310
00:09:22,545 --> 00:09:24,930
[A window blind with its slats open, sunlight streaming through. Beside it, the Sun in a ring of the same slats, edge-on, light pouring between them.]

311
00:09:22,645 --> 00:09:24,810
MR. ITO: It was always there.

312
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:28,230
[Continues.]

313
00:09:25,180 --> 00:09:28,360
MR. ITO: Edge-on, like the slats of a blind.

314
00:09:28,630 --> 00:09:31,780
[Every slat turns at once. The window goes dark. The Sun’s light is trapped inside.]

315
00:09:28,730 --> 00:09:33,510
MR. ITO: On the morning the Sun went out, every slat turned.

316
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:36,440
[Mira, sharp.]

317
00:09:34,280 --> 00:09:36,320
MIRA: You've seen those lines before.

318
00:09:36,590 --> 00:09:39,740
[Ito, not meeting her eyes. He puts the photograph back in the bag.]

319
00:09:36,690 --> 00:09:41,385
MR. ITO: Once. A long time ago. Nobody listened.

320
00:09:42,055 --> 00:09:45,205
[The two of them in the lamplight. Mira watches his back.]

321
00:09:42,155 --> 00:09:46,295
NARRATOR: I believed him. It was true. It just wasn't all of it.

322
00:09:47,165 --> 00:09:50,315
[Day 100. The heaters’ orange glow fades; frost creeps over the tiles; breath smokes. The lamps stay on.]

323
00:09:47,265 --> 00:09:51,080
NARRATOR: On Day 100, it was fifty below up top.

324
00:09:51,350 --> 00:09:54,500
[Close on the station’s big air heater: its orange glow fades out, and frost creeps across the grille.]

325
00:09:51,450 --> 00:09:55,925
NARRATOR: The heaters that warmed the air coming down into the station froze.

326
00:09:56,195 --> 00:09:58,045
[Ito turns to the family, then follows the engineers toward the frozen air plant, torch in hand.]

327
00:09:56,295 --> 00:09:57,710
MR. ITO: Let me help.

328
00:09:58,195 --> 00:10:01,345
[Reverse: ito answers.]

329
00:09:58,295 --> 00:10:01,660
MR. ITO: I know a little about old machines.

330
00:10:01,930 --> 00:10:04,240
[That night. Under every blanket they own, Kai, Mira and Mum huddle together. Breath smoking even down here.]

331
00:10:02,030 --> 00:10:04,120
NARRATOR: That was the coldest night.

332
00:10:04,390 --> 00:10:07,540
[The station, quiet. People sit close together in the cold, not talking. A man kneels beside a shivering neighbour.]

333
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:09,595
NARRATOR: All over the world, many people didn't make it through that winter.

334
00:10:10,465 --> 00:10:13,615
[Kai’s face in the cold blue light. For once, no joke.]

335
00:10:10,565 --> 00:10:12,735
NARRATOR: Mira? How does this end?

336
00:10:13,805 --> 00:10:16,705
[Mira, small and certain.]

337
00:10:13,905 --> 00:10:15,985
MIRA: With the Sun coming back.

338
00:10:16,855 --> 00:10:20,005
[Morning. The heaters glow orange again, the frost melts off the tiles, and the whole station cheers.]

339
00:10:16,955 --> 00:10:22,115
NARRATOR: By morning, Mr. Ito and the engineers had the heaters running again.

340
00:10:22,385 --> 00:10:24,235
[Later. Ito at his bench with wires and a little dish. Mira leans in.]

341
00:10:22,485 --> 00:10:23,895
MIRA: What are you building?

342
00:10:24,385 --> 00:10:27,535
[Ito, holding it up, eyes on the ceiling as if he can see through it.]

343
00:10:24,485 --> 00:10:27,755
MR. ITO: Something to listen with. And to answer.

344
00:10:28,425 --> 00:10:31,575
[Day 300. The station’s big screen: a beam traced up from the Sun to one star in Cassiopeia, ringed.]

345
00:10:28,525 --> 00:10:33,410
NEWS ANCHOR: After months of searching, astronomers have found where the Sun's light is going.

346
00:10:33,680 --> 00:10:36,830
[The beam reaches the star; a red ring and its name: HD 219134, 21 light-years.]

347
00:10:33,780 --> 00:10:39,520
NEWS ANCHOR: One beam, aimed at a star called HD 219134.

348
00:10:39,790 --> 00:10:42,940
[The station crowd, from behind, watching the big screen over the platform: a glowing speck inside the beam. Ito slowly turns around.]

349
00:10:39,890 --> 00:10:43,955
NEWS ANCHOR: And there's something inside it, coming toward us and slowing down.

350
00:10:44,625 --> 00:10:47,260
[In the crowd under the screen, Ito goes white. Barely a whisper.]

351
00:10:44,725 --> 00:10:46,140
MR. ITO: They're coming.

352
00:10:47,410 --> 00:10:50,560
[The news: the beam, the star, and a glowing speck inside the beam labelled SOMETHING INSIDE · COMING THIS WAY · SLOWING.]

353
00:10:47,510 --> 00:10:50,875
NEWS ANCHOR: At this rate, it will stop in sixty-five days.

354
00:10:51,145 --> 00:10:54,295
[Continues.]

355
00:10:51,245 --> 00:10:54,460
NEWS ANCHOR: Scientists hope the Sun will come back when it does.

356
00:10:55,130 --> 00:10:57,565
[Kai and Mira beside him. Mira watches Ito. He says nothing more.]

357
00:10:55,230 --> 00:10:57,145
NARRATOR: I didn't ask who he meant.

358
00:10:57,715 --> 00:11:00,865
[The shell from far away, a needle of light leaving it toward one faint star.]

359
00:10:57,815 --> 00:11:02,870
NARRATOR: All the Sun's light, pushing against one small thing, coming our way.

360
00:11:03,740 --> 00:11:06,390
[At the transmitter. His finger hovers over the key. He looks at the chart: 21.]

361
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:07,990
[He presses it, once.]

362
00:11:08,140 --> 00:11:11,290
[Day 365, 6:20 a.m. Up the frozen escalator steps: Kai hauls Mr. Ito’s big transmitter dish on his back, step by step, Mira behind him.]

363
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:14,355
NARRATOR: On Day 365, the escalators to the surface had frozen solid.

364
00:11:14,625 --> 00:11:17,775
[Closer: Kai, red-faced, climbing step after frozen step with the dish, without a word.]

365
00:11:14,725 --> 00:11:17,785
NARRATOR: Kai carried Mr. Ito's dish all the way up.

366
00:11:18,055 --> 00:11:20,240
[Reverse: narrator answers.]

367
00:11:18,155 --> 00:11:20,120
NARRATOR: He didn't complain once.

368
00:11:20,390 --> 00:11:23,050
[The surface. All of them in full cold gear, tiny under the brightest Milky Way anyone has ever seen; the dish on its tripod beside them.]

369
00:11:20,490 --> 00:11:22,930
NARRATOR: Half the station came up with us.

370
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:26,350
[Closer: the family bundled in every layer they own, breath freezing in the starlight.]

371
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:26,940
NARRATOR: It was seventy below, so we wore everything we owned.

372
00:11:27,210 --> 00:11:29,440
[Mira grins up at Kai.]

373
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:29,320
MIRA: One whole lap around the Sun.

374
00:11:29,590 --> 00:11:31,470
[Reverse: mira answers.]

375
00:11:29,690 --> 00:11:31,350
MIRA: Happy birthday, Earth!

376
00:11:31,620 --> 00:11:34,770
[Ito checks his watch: 6:30. He looks east.]

377
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:36,700
MR. ITO: It started at 6:52, exactly one year ago.

378
00:11:36,970 --> 00:11:40,120
[Ito looks east. Mira looks at him.]

379
00:11:37,070 --> 00:11:40,760
MR. ITO: I think it stops at 6:52 today.

380
00:11:41,030 --> 00:11:44,180
[6:31. Ito’s little receiver dish clicks in his hands. His face changes.]

381
00:11:41,130 --> 00:11:45,195
MR. ITO: Listen. Something near the Sun is calling us.

382
00:11:45,465 --> 00:11:47,315
[Pulses: one, then two, then three. Kai counts along.]

383
00:11:45,565 --> 00:11:46,375
NARRATOR: One.

384
00:11:47,465 --> 00:11:49,315
[Reverse: kai answers.]

385
00:11:47,565 --> 00:11:48,125
NARRATOR: Two.

386
00:11:49,465 --> 00:11:51,315
[Reverse: kai answers.]

387
00:11:49,565 --> 00:11:50,275
NARRATOR: Three!

388
00:11:51,465 --> 00:11:53,975
[The receiver’s screen. The counter passes three and keeps climbing, faster and faster.]

389
00:11:51,565 --> 00:11:53,355
NARRATOR: Then it didn't stop.

390
00:11:54,125 --> 00:11:56,785
[Kai stops counting.]

391
00:11:54,225 --> 00:11:56,665
NARRATOR: That's not three. That's hundreds.

392
00:11:56,935 --> 00:12:00,085
[Ito, reading the counter as it stops.]

393
00:11:57,035 --> 00:12:00,805
MR. ITO: It's stopped. And listen: they come in rows.

394
00:12:01,075 --> 00:12:04,225
[Reverse: ito answers.]

395
00:12:01,175 --> 00:12:04,065
MR. ITO: Every row is the same length.

396
00:12:04,635 --> 00:12:06,515
[Kai, deadpan.]

397
00:12:04,735 --> 00:12:06,195
NARRATOR: Rows of what?

398
00:12:06,665 --> 00:12:09,650
[Ito, not looking up.]

399
00:12:06,765 --> 00:12:09,530
MR. ITO: Loud beeps and soft beeps.

400
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:12,950
[Reverse: ito answers.]

401
00:12:09,900 --> 00:12:13,855
MR. ITO: Like squares that are filled in, or left empty.

402
00:12:14,425 --> 00:12:17,575
[Ito, not looking up.]

403
00:12:14,525 --> 00:12:18,415
MR. ITO: We sent a message like this ourselves, once.

404
00:12:18,685 --> 00:12:21,835
[Reverse: ito answers.]

405
00:12:18,785 --> 00:12:21,525
MR. ITO: In 1974.

406
00:12:22,095 --> 00:12:25,245
[Mira’s eyes go wide. She is the artist.]

407
00:12:22,195 --> 00:12:26,090
MIRA: Filled squares and empty squares? That's just graph paper.

408
00:12:26,660 --> 00:12:28,645
[Reverse: mira answers.]

409
00:12:26,760 --> 00:12:28,025
MIRA: It's a picture!

410
00:12:28,795 --> 00:12:31,945
[The pulses fall into a grid, 23 across and 73 down, filling row by row.]

411
00:12:28,895 --> 00:12:31,740
MR. ITO: Row by row. Let's see it.

412
00:12:32,795 --> 00:12:35,945
[The finished picture, framed at the top: rows of dots counting one to ten.]

413
00:12:32,895 --> 00:12:36,350
NARRATOR: At the top: counting, from one to ten.

414
00:12:36,620 --> 00:12:39,770
[Lower: a tall figure with a crest and three-fingered hands. Beside it, small, a human.]

415
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:40,890
NARRATOR: Then one of them. And one of us, beside them, much smaller.

416
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:44,710
[Lower still: their star with its planets, one raised; the beam; our Sun inside its shell; Earth.]

417
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:45,455
NARRATOR: Their star. And their ship, parked beside our Sun.

418
00:12:46,325 --> 00:12:48,660
[Mira turns to Ito. He is staring at the little screen.]

419
00:12:46,425 --> 00:12:48,540
MIRA: Mr. Ito? What is it?

420
00:12:48,810 --> 00:12:51,245
[Ito, quietly.]

421
00:12:48,910 --> 00:12:50,725
MR. ITO: It's a reply.

422
00:12:51,395 --> 00:12:53,245
[Mira, not understanding.]

423
00:12:51,495 --> 00:12:52,510
MIRA: To who?

424
00:12:53,395 --> 00:12:55,980
[Ito, finally.]

425
00:12:53,495 --> 00:12:54,860
MR. ITO: To me.

426
00:12:56,130 --> 00:12:59,280
[The Morse key again. One… two-two… three-three-three: the same rhythm as the pulses.]

427
00:12:59,730 --> 00:13:02,880
[He walks out of the dome. Over the far ridge, the sky goes gold, and the Sun rises.]

428
00:13:04,330 --> 00:13:06,380
[Close: his hand on the dome railing in the sunrise. Tap. Tap. Tap.]

429
00:13:06,530 --> 00:13:09,380
[His face in the sunrise, round glasses glinting.]

430
00:13:09,530 --> 00:13:12,680
[Match cut: the same face, forty-three years older, in starlight, tapping his receiver three times.]

431
00:13:09,630 --> 00:13:15,455
NARRATOR: The young man in the observatory was Mr. Ito, in 1983.

432
00:13:16,125 --> 00:13:19,275
[The star chart again: the three lines meeting at one star. 21.]

433
00:13:16,225 --> 00:13:20,995
NARRATOR: He found lines in old eclipse photos. They pointed to one star.

434
00:13:21,265 --> 00:13:23,675
[Ito turns to Mira and tells her, plainly.]

435
00:13:21,365 --> 00:13:23,555
MR. ITO: It was a signpost.

436
00:13:23,825 --> 00:13:26,975
[Reverse: ito answers.]

437
00:13:23,925 --> 00:13:28,790
MR. ITO: It meant: if you can see this, call us. So I did.

438
00:13:29,060 --> 00:13:32,210
[His young hand on the key.]

439
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:32,325
NARRATOR: The astronomers he worked for told him not to.

440
00:13:32,595 --> 00:13:35,745
[Continues.]

441
00:13:32,695 --> 00:13:35,740
NARRATOR: He knocked anyway. One. Two. Three.

442
00:13:36,410 --> 00:13:39,560
[The top of their picture again: the counting rows, one to ten.]

443
00:13:36,510 --> 00:13:41,890
MR. ITO: And they counted on, all the way to ten, so I'd know it was them.

444
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:45,310
[Earth and a star 21 light-years apart. The knock crosses out: 1983 → 2004. The answer crosses back: 2004 → 2026.]

445
00:13:42,260 --> 00:13:45,295
NARRATOR: His knock took twenty-one years to reach them.

446
00:13:45,565 --> 00:13:48,715
[Continues.]

447
00:13:45,665 --> 00:13:50,710
NARRATOR: They set off the day it arrived. It took them twenty-three years to get here.

448
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:54,130
[Diagram: their ship with a huge sail, arriving at almost light speed; the beam of all the Sun’s light pushing on the sail from the front; the speed bar falling to zero over twelve months.]

449
00:13:51,080 --> 00:13:54,645
MR. ITO: Something that fast can't stop by itself.

450
00:13:57,380 --> 00:14:00,530
[Continues.]

451
00:13:57,480 --> 00:14:02,700
MR. ITO: It needs a push from the front. Only a whole star pushes that hard.

452
00:14:02,970 --> 00:14:06,120
[The shell around the Sun, slowly turning.]

453
00:14:03,070 --> 00:14:09,400
MR. ITO: They built that shell long ago, as a harbour, so one day they could stop here.

454
00:14:09,670 --> 00:14:12,255
[Continues.]

455
00:14:09,770 --> 00:14:12,135
MR. ITO: How, nobody knows.

456
00:14:12,405 --> 00:14:15,555
[Ito looks east, where the Sun should be.]

457
00:14:12,505 --> 00:14:16,410
MR. ITO: So for one year, our Sun was their brake.

458
00:14:16,680 --> 00:14:19,830
[Ito turns back to the family.]

459
00:14:16,780 --> 00:14:19,760
MR. ITO: In a few minutes they'll have stopped.

460
00:14:20,030 --> 00:14:22,990
[Reverse: ito answers.]

461
00:14:20,130 --> 00:14:22,870
MR. ITO: And they won't need it any more.

462
00:14:23,140 --> 00:14:26,290
[Ito, quietly.]

463
00:14:23,240 --> 00:14:26,820
MR. ITO: I knew they might come, any year now.

464
00:14:27,090 --> 00:14:30,240
[Reverse: ito answers.]

465
00:14:27,190 --> 00:14:30,105
MR. ITO: That's why my bag was packed.

466
00:14:30,775 --> 00:14:32,780
[Mira, frowning, asks the obvious question.]

467
00:14:30,875 --> 00:14:32,660
MIRA: Then why didn't they warn us?

468
00:14:32,930 --> 00:14:36,080
[Ito, gently.]

469
00:14:33,030 --> 00:14:37,220
MR. ITO: Because a warning can't travel faster than light.

470
00:14:37,690 --> 00:14:40,475
[Wider: Mira takes that in; Mum puts a hand on her shoulder.]

471
00:14:37,790 --> 00:14:40,355
MR. ITO: They were almost that fast.

472
00:14:40,625 --> 00:14:43,775
[Reverse: ito answers.]

473
00:14:40,725 --> 00:14:43,315
MR. ITO: It would have arrived with them.

474
00:14:43,985 --> 00:14:46,710
[Mira asks the hardest question.]

475
00:14:44,085 --> 00:14:46,190
MIRA: Did they know it would hurt us?

476
00:14:46,860 --> 00:14:49,470
[Ito has no answer.]

477
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:48,550
MR. ITO: I don't know.

478
00:14:49,620 --> 00:14:52,770
[Mira reaches out to him.]

479
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:52,460
MR. ITO: I'm so sorry, Mira.

480
00:14:53,730 --> 00:14:56,170
[Reverse: mira answers.]

481
00:14:53,830 --> 00:14:56,050
MIRA: It's okay. You didn't know.

482
00:14:56,320 --> 00:14:59,470
[The picture again. The camera slides to the bottom: an empty square, pulsing.]

483
00:14:56,420 --> 00:15:00,175
NARRATOR: At the bottom of their picture was an empty square.

484
00:15:00,845 --> 00:15:03,005
[Mira, very still.]

485
00:15:00,945 --> 00:15:02,285
MIRA: It's our turn.

486
00:15:03,155 --> 00:15:06,305
[On every phone: radio observatories around the world, all showing the same pulses.]

487
00:15:03,255 --> 00:15:07,460
NARRATOR: Every radio telescope on Earth got the same picture.

488
00:15:07,930 --> 00:15:11,080
[On every phone: feeds from capitals, crowds outside parliaments, placards, arguments, all at the same minute.]

489
00:15:08,030 --> 00:15:11,640
NARRATOR: Every government started arguing about what to say back.

490
00:15:12,210 --> 00:15:15,230
[Mira takes the folded drawing out of her pocket and holds it up to Ito.]

491
00:15:13,360 --> 00:15:15,110
MIRA: Can we send this?

492
00:15:15,380 --> 00:15:18,530
[Ito looks at the drawing for a long moment.]

493
00:15:15,480 --> 00:15:18,420
MR. ITO: Yes. I think we can.

494
00:15:19,090 --> 00:15:22,240
[Mira’s crayon sun under Ito’s lamp, then the same picture as squares: a sun with rays, a little girl standing under it.]

495
00:15:19,190 --> 00:15:22,070
NARRATOR: My drawing, from the morning it went out.

496
00:15:23,690 --> 00:15:26,840
[Continues.]

497
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:27,655
NARRATOR: A big yellow sun. And me, standing under it.

498
00:15:28,425 --> 00:15:30,905
[Kai, suddenly very sixteen. Ito, perfectly calm, sets his little receiver down and steps to the transmitter.]

499
00:15:28,525 --> 00:15:30,785
NARRATOR: Are we even allowed to do this?

500
00:15:31,055 --> 00:15:33,590
[Reverse: ito answers.]

501
00:15:31,155 --> 00:15:32,870
MR. ITO: Probably not.

502
00:15:33,740 --> 00:15:36,890
[6:47. Ito presses the key. The transmitter light goes red.]

503
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:38,070
NARRATOR: He pressed the key, the way he had in 1983.

504
00:15:38,340 --> 00:15:40,650
[Wide. Five small figures in the snow, the transmitter glowing. A clock counting down to 6:52.]

505
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:40,130
NARRATOR: Five minutes to go.

506
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:43,950
[All over the world, people on rooftops in the dark, looking up at the same patch of sky.]

507
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:44,540
NARRATOR: The whole world was watching the same patch of sky.

508
00:15:44,810 --> 00:15:46,660
[Mira, scared. Mum pulls her in.]

509
00:15:44,910 --> 00:15:46,295
MIRA: What if he's wrong?

510
00:15:46,810 --> 00:15:49,875
[Reverse: mum answers.]

511
00:15:46,910 --> 00:15:49,455
NARRATOR: Then we'll wait. Together.

512
00:15:50,025 --> 00:15:53,175
[Ito, eyes on the east. He taps the transmitter three times.]

513
00:15:55,025 --> 00:15:58,175
[Space. The shell’s slats turn, all together, edge-on. Light pours out between them.]

514
00:15:59,225 --> 00:16:01,480
[Low in the east, the Sun is simply there. Gold light floods across the snow and their faces.]

515
00:15:59,325 --> 00:16:00,560
MIRA: There it is!

516
00:16:01,630 --> 00:16:04,780
[Earth from space: the whole day side lights up at once.]

517
00:16:01,730 --> 00:16:06,355
NARRATOR: Everywhere it was daytime, the Sun came back at the same moment.

518
00:16:06,625 --> 00:16:08,675
[Sydney, 3:52 PM: the surfer on the frozen beach, in the sun again.]

519
00:16:08,825 --> 00:16:10,675
[Chennai, 11:22 AM: the farmer in the frosted paddy, arms up.]

520
00:16:10,825 --> 00:16:12,675
[Manila: the boy at the bus stop, laughing in the light.]

521
00:16:12,825 --> 00:16:15,855
[The news: New York at night, still dark, a city waiting for an ordinary sunrise.]

522
00:16:12,925 --> 00:16:15,735
NARRATOR: In New York it was the middle of the night.

523
00:16:16,005 --> 00:16:19,155
[Continues.]

524
00:16:16,105 --> 00:16:20,505
NARRATOR: They got their sunrise a few hours later, the ordinary way.

525
00:16:21,175 --> 00:16:24,325
[The family in the sunlight. Mira holds her drawing up beside the real Sun.]

526
00:16:21,275 --> 00:16:27,250
NARRATOR: At 6:52, exactly one year after it went out, we were standing in sunlight.

527
00:16:28,520 --> 00:16:31,670
[A memory, warm as home movie: the same roof, every dawn for forty-three years. Mr. Ito, young, then greying, then old, taps his telescope three times as the Sun comes up.]

528
00:16:28,620 --> 00:16:33,900
NARRATOR: Every morning for forty-three years, Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

529
00:16:34,770 --> 00:16:37,920
[Close on his face in the early light.]

530
00:16:34,870 --> 00:16:37,980
NARRATOR: I used to think it was because he liked it.

531
00:16:39,050 --> 00:16:42,200
[Wider: the old man alone on the roof at dawn, tapping the telescope three times.]

532
00:16:39,150 --> 00:16:41,555
NARRATOR: He was waiting for an answer.

533
00:16:43,025 --> 00:16:46,175
[7:03. Ito’s little receiver clicks again: one… two… three.]

534
00:16:43,125 --> 00:16:47,700
NARRATOR: Sixteen minutes after we sent my drawing, the dish clicked again.

535
00:16:50,425 --> 00:16:53,575
[The end screen: the shell turning, its three pulses, THE REPLY, and room for the subscribe and next-video cards.]

536
00:16:50,525 --> 00:16:53,465
NARRATOR: What would you have sent? Tell us in the comments.
